00:06:08.285 --> 00:06:09.853
OK, so how do we do this?

00:06:09.853 --> 00:06:13.757
How do I tell you what
shape that should be?

00:06:13.757 --> 00:06:18.995
Well, we have a way to do
this, and it's got a name even.

00:06:21.631 --> 00:06:27.504
So just to contrast,
Lewis is what we did last.

00:06:27.504 --> 00:06:35.879
And Lewis gives me the
numbers and type of bonds, OK.

00:06:39.082 --> 00:06:40.550
The number and type of bonds.

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:43.220
But now, I've got
a way that's called

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:49.593
Valence-Shell Electron-Pair
Repulsion model, VSEPR, VSEPR.

00:06:49.593 --> 00:06:54.231
But see, chemists are--
if I haven't conveyed this

00:06:54.231 --> 00:06:58.702
before, chemists are really
good at naming things.

00:06:58.702 --> 00:07:06.009
So it could be valence electron
shell pair repulsion model.

00:07:06.009 --> 00:07:06.676
It's not.

00:07:06.676 --> 00:07:07.577
But it could be.

00:07:07.577 --> 00:07:09.646
And if it weren't
we'd call it VESPR.

00:07:09.646 --> 00:07:11.014
It would sound a lot better.

00:07:11.014 --> 00:07:13.383
Right, and so that's
what we call it

00:07:13.383 --> 00:07:16.219
even though we write VSEPR.

00:07:16.219 --> 00:07:18.855
So we write it like
this, but we say vesper,

00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:21.224
because this is chemistry.

00:07:24.094 --> 00:07:25.395
And this gives us the shape.

00:07:25.395 --> 00:07:33.103
Now, it's based on actually a
very straightforward premise,

00:07:33.103 --> 00:07:37.240
which is that electrons repel
each other, something we

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.576
already knew.

00:07:39.576 --> 00:07:54.024
OK, electrons repel each other,
and the stable arrangement--

00:07:54.024 --> 00:07:56.058
I hear that molecule
changing shape.

00:07:56.058 --> 00:07:58.929
It makes me happy.

00:07:58.929 --> 00:08:04.534
OK, the stable
arrangement minimizes--

00:08:04.534 --> 00:08:06.503
now, this makes sense right?

00:08:06.503 --> 00:08:09.673
Minimizes the repulsions.

00:08:09.673 --> 00:08:13.009
OK, that's really the
whole premise of VSEPR.

00:08:13.009 --> 00:08:15.612
VSEPR theory is a way--
it's a very simple recipe

00:08:15.612 --> 00:08:20.750
that we will learn and apply
that is based on this premise.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:22.118
We already talked
about electrons

00:08:22.118 --> 00:08:26.256
repelling each other in
atoms, right, and in a bond.

00:08:26.256 --> 00:08:28.425
And now we use the same
idea that electrons

00:08:28.425 --> 00:08:32.729
repel each other for a molecule,
right, within a molecule.

00:08:32.729 --> 00:08:34.164
OK, good.

00:08:34.164 --> 00:08:35.799
But we need to rank order this.

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:37.000
We need to rank order this.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.969
And so we have a rank order.

00:08:38.969 --> 00:08:45.375
And you'll see kind
of why, as we go.

00:08:45.375 --> 00:08:46.776
I'll tell it to you right now.

00:08:46.776 --> 00:08:48.445
And I need
abbreviations, because I

00:08:48.445 --> 00:08:51.515
don't want to keep writing
bonding pair and lone pair.

00:08:51.515 --> 00:08:55.785
So I'm going to say that a
bonding pair, a bonding pair,

00:08:55.785 --> 00:08:56.920
oh what's a bonding pair?

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:00.724
Well, it's the two electrons
in a bond right there.

00:09:00.724 --> 00:09:02.259
That's a bonding pair.

00:09:02.259 --> 00:09:04.060
OK, good.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:09.966
So a bonding pair, to save time,
we're going to write as a BP.

00:09:09.966 --> 00:09:13.370
And a lone pair,
what's a long pair?

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:15.539
Well, we already know that.

00:09:15.539 --> 00:09:20.577
A lone pair is right there.

00:09:20.577 --> 00:09:24.147
A non-bonding pair,
a lone pair, an LP.

00:09:24.147 --> 00:09:25.081
OK, good.

00:09:25.081 --> 00:09:31.288
Now, this allows me, now that
I have this very important key,

00:09:31.288 --> 00:09:33.189
I can tell you
what the repulsion

00:09:33.189 --> 00:09:37.427
order is without writing out
bonding and lone all the time.

00:09:37.427 --> 00:09:43.700
Repulsion order and this
is what we follow in VSEPR.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:49.306
OK, so we're going to go the
lowest repulsion is between two

00:09:49.306 --> 00:09:53.510
bonding pairs, bonding
pair to bonding pair.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:58.248
And the medium medium, OK,
so the next in our list

00:09:58.248 --> 00:10:06.756
is bonding pair to lone
pair, and the highest

00:10:06.756 --> 00:10:09.526
is between two lone pairs.

00:10:09.526 --> 00:10:13.663
All I'm telling you
is in this ordering

00:10:13.663 --> 00:10:18.602
is that OK, electrons repel
each other, yeah, got it,

00:10:18.602 --> 00:10:20.604
but there's an ordering to it.

00:10:20.604 --> 00:10:23.673
If I'm a lone pair, and
I see another lone pair,

00:10:23.673 --> 00:10:25.675
that I'm more repelled.

00:10:25.675 --> 00:10:27.911
Those two things are
more repelled, then

00:10:27.911 --> 00:10:29.613
a lone pair and a
bonding pair, which

00:10:29.613 --> 00:10:31.581
are more repelled than
two bonding pairs,

00:10:31.581 --> 00:10:33.583
and we got one more thing
we got to think about,

00:10:33.583 --> 00:10:38.955
because you see the bonding
pairs can be single, double,

00:10:38.955 --> 00:10:40.724
or triple.

00:10:40.724 --> 00:10:43.059
And so those have a
rank order, which also

00:10:43.059 --> 00:10:45.929
makes a lot of sense, right.

00:10:45.929 --> 00:10:52.736
So like one bonding pair
would have a single bond.

00:10:52.736 --> 00:10:55.105
With a single bond would
have a lower repulsion

00:10:55.105 --> 00:11:03.046
than a double bond, which
would have less repulsion

00:11:03.046 --> 00:11:05.482
than a triple bond, right?

00:11:05.482 --> 00:11:07.617
But that makes a
lot of sense, right?

00:11:07.617 --> 00:11:11.388
You're putting more
electrons in the bond.

00:11:11.388 --> 00:11:14.224
And so there's more
stuff to repel.

00:11:14.224 --> 00:11:16.726
Right, so if I'm talking
about bonding pairs,

00:11:16.726 --> 00:11:19.396
there is also a sub order.

00:11:19.396 --> 00:11:20.764
OK, that makes sense, right?

00:11:20.764 --> 00:11:27.470
So this is like a single bond
line, two lines, no room,

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:29.372
three lines, right?

00:11:31.741 --> 00:11:32.242
All right.

00:11:32.242 --> 00:11:36.746
Now this frames the VSEPR model.

00:11:36.746 --> 00:11:39.683
Now let's apply it, and
the rules are actually

00:11:39.683 --> 00:11:41.151
really pretty straightforward.

00:11:41.151 --> 00:11:42.552
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:11:42.552 --> 00:11:45.455
going to build our
understanding by applying this

00:11:45.455 --> 00:11:47.924
to some examples, because
it's exactly the same thing we

00:11:47.924 --> 00:11:50.727
did with Lewis.

00:11:50.727 --> 00:11:54.297
OK, so I'm going to start my
recipe with the first three

00:11:54.297 --> 00:11:55.632
ingredients.

00:11:55.632 --> 00:11:58.568
Passion is always there, too.

00:11:58.568 --> 00:12:01.671
But these are the first three
ingredients of my VSEPR recipe.

00:12:01.671 --> 00:12:03.206
First, I write the structure.

00:12:03.206 --> 00:12:04.174
We know how to do that.

00:12:04.174 --> 00:12:05.408
We learn how to do that.

00:12:05.408 --> 00:12:08.712
And then I'm going to classify
the pairs of electrons

00:12:08.712 --> 00:12:09.813
as bonding or non-bonding.

00:12:09.813 --> 00:12:13.083
So I'm going to just
label them like that.

00:12:13.083 --> 00:12:15.652
And then I'm going to maximize
the separation between domains

00:12:15.652 --> 00:12:19.189
and pay attention
to these rules,

00:12:19.189 --> 00:12:21.324
and I'm going to
start a little bit

00:12:21.324 --> 00:12:24.394
of a table over here, number--

00:12:24.394 --> 00:12:27.263
because we're going to fill
this table out-- number

00:12:27.263 --> 00:12:31.101
of electron regions.

00:12:31.101 --> 00:12:34.037
OK, let's do this.

00:12:34.037 --> 00:12:39.042
You know when I do this,
there's nothing but fun coming.

00:12:39.042 --> 00:12:40.577
Nothing but fun.

00:12:40.577 --> 00:12:42.979
I went all the way,
all the way over.

00:12:42.979 --> 00:12:43.646
Why?

00:12:43.646 --> 00:12:46.282
Because I need some space.

00:12:46.282 --> 00:12:50.920
Now, here, we're going to
say electron pair geometry.

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:54.190
We'll be talking about
this today, geometry.

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:55.091
OK, good.

00:12:57.994 --> 00:13:05.068
And then, here, OK, now this
is sort of the molecular--

00:13:05.068 --> 00:13:09.072
molecular geometry.

00:13:09.072 --> 00:13:12.776
So I'm going from--

00:13:12.776 --> 00:13:16.679
I'm going from counting
electron regions

00:13:16.679 --> 00:13:20.884
and labeling what kind they are
to a geometry of the electron

00:13:20.884 --> 00:13:25.889
