And so three, this is
going to be max separation,

00:14:52.742 --> 00:14:57.747
max sep will have to be linear.

00:14:57.747 --> 00:14:59.215
There's just no
other way about it.

00:14:59.215 --> 00:15:01.718
I've only got two
electron domains.

00:15:01.718 --> 00:15:03.519
I've got no lone pairs.

00:15:03.519 --> 00:15:05.655
So two bonding domains,
two electron domains.

00:15:05.655 --> 00:15:06.689
It's got to be linear.

00:15:06.689 --> 00:15:08.858
That's the only way I can go.

00:15:08.858 --> 00:15:11.394
So if the number of
electron regions is two,

00:15:11.394 --> 00:15:18.668
which it is for BeH2, OK,
and and then the number of--

00:15:18.668 --> 00:15:24.874
the electron pair
geometry is linear, right,

00:15:24.874 --> 00:15:27.310
because that maximizes
the separations.

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:31.547
And there is no lone
pairs, and so it's linear.

00:15:31.547 --> 00:15:35.018
You say, well, that
really seems redundant.

00:15:35.018 --> 00:15:37.353
It is in this case.

00:15:37.353 --> 00:15:42.525
It's not going to stay that
way, not going to stay that way.

00:15:42.525 --> 00:15:43.426
OK.

00:15:43.426 --> 00:15:44.060
Good.

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:46.362
One lone pair, no.

00:15:46.362 --> 00:15:47.897
No lone pairs.

00:15:47.897 --> 00:15:53.469
I mean, if I had two
electron regions total, OK,

00:15:53.469 --> 00:15:56.539
and one of them was a lone
pair, it's kind of boring.

00:15:56.539 --> 00:15:58.540
I mean, you know, if this
were a lone pair here,

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:00.944
instead of a bond, well,
clearly it's linear.

00:16:00.944 --> 00:16:03.046
There's only two atoms.

00:16:03.046 --> 00:16:05.081
That's kind of
the simplest case.

00:16:05.081 --> 00:16:08.451
We won't really talk about that.

00:16:08.451 --> 00:16:08.952
Good.

00:16:08.952 --> 00:16:11.621
OK, now it's the
same kind of thing.

00:16:11.621 --> 00:16:14.791
Let me just make sure that
