00:27:39.808 --> 00:27:41.443
You have to put your
phone down and be

00:27:41.443 --> 00:27:43.979
there to have this moment.

00:27:43.979 --> 00:27:48.283
And I highly encourage you
to be inspired by this.

00:27:48.283 --> 00:27:51.353
Now, smell and taste
are actually related.

00:27:51.353 --> 00:27:55.090
And smell is, you know, it's
actually a fascinating thing.

00:27:55.090 --> 00:28:00.863
We can smell about
10,000 different smells.

00:28:00.863 --> 00:28:02.131
It's remarkable.

00:28:02.131 --> 00:28:06.502
A dog can smell between
10 and 100,000 times more.

00:28:06.502 --> 00:28:09.872
Right, so if you do the analogy
that people do with vision,

00:28:09.872 --> 00:28:11.073
we can see a third of a mile.

00:28:11.073 --> 00:28:13.475
A dog can see about 3,000 miles.

00:28:13.475 --> 00:28:17.513
Right, that's pretty cool.

00:28:17.513 --> 00:28:19.381
But the question
is why do we smell?

00:28:19.381 --> 00:28:20.749
How do we smell?

00:28:20.749 --> 00:28:22.251
How do we taste?

00:28:22.251 --> 00:28:25.087
And it turns out that the
way that we distinguish

00:28:25.087 --> 00:28:27.589
from one smell and
one taste and another

00:28:27.589 --> 00:28:29.758
has to do with the
shape of the molecule.

00:28:29.758 --> 00:28:33.228
And so just like a
key right, with a key,

00:28:33.228 --> 00:28:34.763
it's all of the same material.

00:28:34.763 --> 00:28:36.231
It's the same
chemistry in the key.

00:28:36.231 --> 00:28:39.101
But the shape is different that
can unlock the door or not.

00:28:39.101 --> 00:28:41.804
That is literally how
our receptor cells

00:28:41.804 --> 00:28:44.640
work for taste and smell.

00:28:44.640 --> 00:28:46.542
This is a cartoon I found
that I kind of like.

00:28:46.542 --> 00:28:51.380
