00:29:05.494 --> 00:29:06.628
But it's shape dependent.

00:29:06.628 --> 00:29:08.497
Now the thing is, taste
works that way, too.

00:29:08.497 --> 00:29:11.900
So this is what's
happening in your tongue.

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:14.203
You've got these taste buds.

00:29:14.203 --> 00:29:17.139
We've all heard the
word taste bud, right?

00:29:17.139 --> 00:29:19.708
But what is really going on?

00:29:19.708 --> 00:29:23.645
What is really going on is
a combination of chemistry

00:29:23.645 --> 00:29:26.815
and shape recognition.

00:29:26.815 --> 00:29:28.851
So the taste bud, if you
look at the taste bud,

00:29:28.851 --> 00:29:32.488
that's inside of the little
pores inside your tongue.

00:29:32.488 --> 00:29:33.655
There's a blow up of it.

00:29:33.655 --> 00:29:35.791
And so this is what the surface
of your tongue looks like.

00:29:35.791 --> 00:29:36.959
And there's a little pore.

00:29:36.959 --> 00:29:38.560
And there's little
filters in there

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:41.063
that helps certain molecules
kind of get in there.

00:29:41.063 --> 00:29:43.465
And what happens when
they get in there.

00:29:43.465 --> 00:29:46.735
Well, what happens is you've got
these taste receptor cells that

00:29:46.735 --> 00:29:48.470
are like lock key pairs.

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:51.640
They actually only look at
like the circle or the diamond,

00:29:51.640 --> 00:29:54.143
and they can tell you
which one is which.

00:29:54.143 --> 00:29:58.080
And that's a major part of how
we distinguish from one shape

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:01.583
to another, and
it's actually so--

00:30:01.583 --> 00:30:04.052
it goes back a long way.

00:30:04.052 --> 00:30:04.853
Why?

00:30:04.853 --> 00:30:10.859
Because shape and smell
are literally survival.

00:30:10.859 --> 00:30:12.427
They are literally
like you can--

00:30:12.427 --> 00:30:18.300
if you taste something, and it
tastes poisonous, don't eat it.

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:19.501
You live.

00:30:19.501 --> 00:30:21.036
Right?

00:30:21.036 --> 00:30:24.807
So it is a very emotional
thing to smell and taste,

00:30:24.807 --> 00:30:28.377
because it is actually
coupled to your very survival.

00:30:28.377 --> 00:30:32.581
And Democritus
himself, Democritus,

00:30:32.581 --> 00:30:37.152
our friend Democritus, said
that shape must be involved.

00:30:37.152 --> 00:30:39.888
He thought that because
things that taste bitter

00:30:39.888 --> 00:30:43.992
are sharp that the bitter
molecules were sharp.

00:30:43.992 --> 00:30:47.796
They must have sharpness to
them, like shards of glass.

00:30:47.796 --> 00:30:48.764
That's how he imagined.

00:30:48.764 --> 00:30:50.532
And the sweet
molecules were sort

00:30:50.532 --> 00:30:54.136
of the soft, fluffy spheres.

00:30:54.136 --> 00:30:57.306
That's not that far
off from being sort

00:30:57.306 --> 00:31:01.877
of what happens a little bit.

00:31:01.877 --> 00:31:06.114
But when we look at it like,
you know, glucose and quinine,

00:31:06.114 --> 00:31:09.885
we say, OK, those are very
different chemistries.

00:31:09.885 --> 00:31:13.155
So it might not be as
obvious why one of them

00:31:13.155 --> 00:31:15.490
taste so different
than the other.

00:31:15.490 --> 00:31:17.826
But check out this example.

00:31:17.826 --> 00:31:20.863
This is the same molecule,
the carvone molecule

00:31:20.863 --> 00:31:22.631
that is called an
enantiomer, which

00:31:22.631 --> 00:31:24.733
means that it has handedness.

00:31:24.733 --> 00:31:27.302
It's the exact same
chemical formula

00:31:27.302 --> 00:31:30.472
and the exact same structure,
except for one is like this,

00:31:30.472 --> 00:31:32.307
and one is like that.

00:31:32.307 --> 00:31:33.876
That's handedness.

00:31:33.876 --> 00:31:34.643
Right.

00:31:34.643 --> 00:31:38.413
And that difference
makes one of them

00:31:38.413 --> 00:31:40.949
taste and smell like spearmint
and the other like caraway

00:31:40.949 --> 00:31:42.417
seeds, right?

00:31:42.417 --> 00:31:44.987
It's incredible.

00:31:44.987 --> 00:31:46.788
It means that in our
tongues in our noses

00:31:46.788 --> 00:31:52.027
we must have chiral, the ability
to determine the chirality.

00:31:52.027 --> 00:31:53.295
It's pretty cool.

00:31:53.295 --> 00:31:53.795
Right.

00:31:53.795 --> 00:31:56.798
So shape is critical, and
this is one example of why.

00:31:56.798 --> 00:31:57.900
This is one example of why.

00:31:57.900 --> 00:31:59.835
OK.

00:31:59.835 --> 00:32:03.405
Back to my VSEPR recipe.

00:32:03.405 --> 00:32:06.808
Now, we've got to go a
little further, because this

00:32:06.808 --> 00:32:11.213
is three electron domains,
three electron domains.

00:32:11.213 --> 00:32:13.415
They can be messed around.

00:32:13.415 --> 00:32:15.150
They can be messed around.

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:16.718
Right, especially
in this table, we're

00:32:16.718 --> 00:32:19.187
talking about lone pairs or not.

00:32:19.187 --> 00:32:21.823
OK, I gave you the example,
which I kind of fudged

00:32:21.823 --> 00:32:25.527
in there with bond order.

00:32:25.527 --> 00:32:27.596
But what happens
if I go to four?

00:32:27.596 --> 00:32:31.333
So if I go to four, let's
do an example with four--

00:32:37.272 --> 00:32:41.610
if I go to four, I
need some room here.

00:32:41.610 --> 00:32:43.879
Why don't I do it
in the center here?

00:32:48.650 --> 00:32:52.854
Let's do an example
of all possibilities

00:32:52.854 --> 00:32:54.723
with four, with four domains.

00:32:54.723 --> 00:32:56.992
And there's three really
good examples, right?

00:32:56.992 --> 00:32:59.394
And you know them.

00:32:59.394 --> 00:33:03.699
Let's do CH4.

00:33:03.699 --> 00:33:06.902
So we'll do these kind
of more quickly, CH4--

00:33:06.902 --> 00:33:09.438
gesundheit-- which will be CH--

00:33:12.641 --> 00:33:14.910
never leave enough room on top.

00:33:14.910 --> 00:33:18.080
And H. There it is.

00:33:18.080 --> 00:33:18.914
OK.

00:33:18.914 --> 00:33:20.415
Anyway.

00:33:20.415 --> 00:33:21.950
And NH3.

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:36.431
OK, so N H H H. And
H20 to all O H H. This

00:33:36.431 --> 00:33:38.233
is how I drew it
on the first slide.

00:33:38.233 --> 00:33:41.703
Or this is how I drew
it up there before.

00:33:41.703 --> 00:33:45.007
Now the thing is that in
each of these cases, what

00:33:45.007 --> 00:33:47.642
I want you to see in
these three examples is

00:33:47.642 --> 00:33:50.112
their similarity first.

00:33:50.112 --> 00:33:54.683
And their similarity is that if
I pick my central atom, carbon,

00:33:54.683 --> 00:33:59.087
nitrogen, oxygen, if I
pick my central atom,

00:33:59.087 --> 00:34:04.059
and I look around it, each
one has four electron domains.

00:34:04.059 --> 00:34:04.593
Right.

00:34:04.593 --> 00:34:09.063
So in this sense, the kind of
overarching electron geometry

00:34:09.063 --> 00:34:12.300
is identical for all
three of those cases.

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:14.902
It's identical.

00:34:14.902 --> 00:34:21.543
And it's tetrahedral,
tetrahedral.

00:34:21.543 --> 00:34:25.880
OK, that's the
electron pair geometry

00:34:25.880 --> 00:34:27.382
that's exactly the same.

00:34:27.382 --> 00:34:28.550
But I need to now count--

00:34:31.286 --> 00:34:32.853
oh, there's the recipe.

00:34:32.853 --> 00:34:34.790
I need to now classify
the electron pair

00:34:34.790 --> 00:34:36.525
as bonding or
non-bonding and then

00:34:36.525 --> 00:34:38.860
maximize the separation
between domains

00:34:38.860 --> 00:34:41.362
while giving more space to
non-bonding domains and bonding

00:34:41.362 --> 00:34:45.300
domains with higher bond order
as we have now talked about.

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:51.806
So if I do that, here, what
I have is four bonding pairs,

00:34:51.806 --> 00:34:55.911
zero, zero lone pairs.

00:34:55.911 --> 00:35:00.682
Here, I've got three bonding
pairs, one lone pair.

00:35:00.682 --> 00:35:04.786
And now I really get to
fill in stuff in my table,

00:35:04.786 --> 00:35:09.858
because now, I've got the
examples of zero, one,

00:35:09.858 --> 00:35:15.330
and two lone pairs for the
same electron domain cloud.

00:35:15.330 --> 00:35:20.769
OK, so there is
zero, one, and two.

00:35:20.769 --> 00:35:25.607
You can see in methane,
OK, the only way for this

00:35:25.607 --> 00:35:27.909
to maximize its repulsion,
they're all the same bonding

00:35:27.909 --> 00:35:30.712
pair, they're all single
bonds, so there's not

00:35:30.712 --> 00:35:32.280
going to be any kind
of funny business

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:36.885
of a double bond pushing harder,
and there's no lone pairs.

00:35:36.885 --> 00:35:39.554
And so this is just going to
have the tetrahedral shape.

00:35:39.554 --> 00:35:41.456
That will be the
shape of the molecule.

00:35:41.456 --> 00:35:44.092
That is the electron
domain shape as well.

00:35:44.092 --> 00:35:53.502
So in this case, it will be
tetrahedral, tetrahedral.

00:35:53.502 --> 00:35:56.872
OK, but see, now,
in this case, I've

00:35:56.872 --> 00:35:59.307
got three bonding pairs
and one lone pair,

00:35:59.307 --> 00:36:03.311
and we know because of the
VSEPR recipe, which has--

00:36:03.311 --> 00:36:04.246
oh, it's up there.

00:36:04.246 --> 00:36:06.515
I keep forgetting it's up there.

00:36:06.515 --> 00:36:08.683
OK we know that I want to
maximize the separation,

00:36:08.683 --> 00:36:10.685
but I got to give more
space to the non-bonding,

00:36:10.685 --> 00:36:14.489
because the lone pair bond
repulsion is stronger.

00:36:14.489 --> 00:36:15.323
Good.

00:36:15.323 --> 00:36:19.594
So what that means is it's going
to be sort of like tetrahedral,

00:36:19.594 --> 00:36:21.363
but in one place,
there is no bond.

00:36:21.363 --> 00:36:23.532
There's just this lone
pair pushing down.

00:36:23.532 --> 00:36:25.634
And so the shape that
you're going to get

00:36:25.634 --> 00:36:34.409
is called trigonal,
trigonal pyramidal.

00:36:41.750 --> 00:36:45.353
OK, because now, that's just
the way that shape looks.

00:36:45.353 --> 00:36:46.555
So this is going to--

00:36:46.555 --> 00:36:48.924
I'll show you actually a
picture of this in a second,

00:36:48.924 --> 00:36:50.358
OK, of what this looks like.

00:36:52.961 --> 00:36:55.931
And then with water, we
now know that this will not

00:36:55.931 --> 00:36:57.832
be the most stable
configuration,

00:36:57.832 --> 00:37:00.402
because these lone pairs
are pushing on these bonds

00:37:00.402 --> 00:37:02.170
too much, so water
is actually going

00:37:02.170 --> 00:37:06.875
to look like this, where
these guys, the lone pairs

00:37:06.875 --> 00:37:10.111
can come out like that, right,
that angles like that, be away

00:37:10.111 --> 00:37:13.481
from each other, right, and
then the bonds are down there,

00:37:13.481 --> 00:37:17.152
and that's going to maximize
the shape of the water--

00:37:17.152 --> 00:37:20.288
I mean, it's going to
minimize the repulsions

00:37:20.288 --> 00:37:22.123
that the electrons
feel in the water.

00:37:22.123 --> 00:37:29.598
And that is back to the
same name, which is bent.

00:37:29.598 --> 00:37:32.500
I've got a different
number of electron domains,

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:36.238
but I wind up with the
same molecular shape.

00:37:36.238 --> 00:37:37.806
See that?

00:37:37.806 --> 00:37:39.341
Bent.

00:37:39.341 --> 00:37:42.510
Now, I know this
is kind of small.

00:37:42.510 --> 00:37:45.847
I've got tables that
have all this at the end

00:37:45.847 --> 00:37:47.115
that you'll have in the slides.

00:37:47.115 --> 00:37:50.552
OK, so I know this is a
little bit crammed in here,

00:37:50.552 --> 00:37:52.287
but the process
of writing this is

00:37:52.287 --> 00:37:56.324
very, very fun and fulfilling.

00:37:56.324 --> 00:37:58.526
Now, we can keep going.

00:37:58.526 --> 00:38:00.729
But I want to tell you about
a labeling system that's

00:38:00.729 --> 00:38:02.564
actually really helpful.

00:38:02.564 --> 00:38:06.901
So if we keep going,
we kind of want

00:38:06.901 --> 00:38:09.404
a system to be able to
think about these things.

00:38:09.404 --> 00:38:11.606
And so there is a system.

00:38:11.606 --> 00:38:12.941
Again, this is chemistry.

00:38:12.941 --> 00:38:14.509
We know how to name things.

00:38:14.509 --> 00:38:16.611
Oh, but I promised you
I'd show you that first.

00:38:16.611 --> 00:38:17.646
So here it is.

00:38:17.646 --> 00:38:18.146
There it is.

00:38:18.146 --> 00:38:18.913
