00:38:18.913 --> 00:38:20.015
I click to that already.

00:38:20.015 --> 00:38:21.816
That's this.

00:38:21.816 --> 00:38:25.153
And the reason I wanted to
show you this, this is from

00:38:25.153 --> 00:38:26.888
[? Avril, ?] your
textbook that you're all

00:38:26.888 --> 00:38:28.556
reading so carefully.

00:38:28.556 --> 00:38:30.091
And look at that.

00:38:30.091 --> 00:38:32.594
This really brings it home.

00:38:32.594 --> 00:38:34.062
Look at that lone pair.

00:38:34.062 --> 00:38:36.264
Now you're seeing
it as the electrons

00:38:36.264 --> 00:38:41.036
feel it, which is a
probability distribution cloud.

00:38:41.036 --> 00:38:42.437
That's the lone
pair, and now you

00:38:42.437 --> 00:38:45.006
can see the lone
pair wants space.

00:38:45.006 --> 00:38:48.977
It wants room, and
it's pushing down

00:38:48.977 --> 00:38:54.149
on these bonds, which are
pushing away from each other.

00:38:54.149 --> 00:38:55.517
And that's how
you can figure out

00:38:55.517 --> 00:38:58.853
what shape it is,
trigonal pyramidal.

00:38:58.853 --> 00:39:03.558
That's trigonal pyramidal,
OK, the molecule.

00:39:07.362 --> 00:39:10.398
Right, as I said, we need a way
to label things in chemistry.

00:39:10.398 --> 00:39:13.401
So we can keep going-- linear,
trigonal planar, tetrahedral,

00:39:13.401 --> 00:39:19.841
trigonal bipyramidal, oh,
five, octahedral, six.

00:39:19.841 --> 00:39:23.878
Don't get confused octa,
eight, but octahedral

00:39:23.878 --> 00:39:28.950
is for six electron
pair domains, right.

00:39:28.950 --> 00:39:30.385
But we need a
system to name this.

00:39:30.385 --> 00:39:33.988
And so the chemists have
brilliantly come up with AXE.

00:39:33.988 --> 00:39:36.458
Now A is the central atom.

00:39:36.458 --> 00:39:40.261
X is the bonding pair regions,
and E is the lone pair regions.

00:39:43.131 --> 00:39:48.636
X and E. OK, and so if
I go back to like BF3,

00:39:48.636 --> 00:39:50.305
well, this would be--

00:39:50.305 --> 00:39:52.741
oh there you go, AX3.

