00:40:36.317 --> 00:40:40.588
It's a highly sophisticated,
advanced labeling system.

00:40:40.588 --> 00:40:43.391
That simply allows
us to keep going,

00:40:43.391 --> 00:40:45.960
to keep going without
writing so many words.

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:48.229
But we know now, A
is the central atom.

00:40:48.229 --> 00:40:49.998
X is the number of
bonding pair regions.

00:40:49.998 --> 00:40:51.833
E is the number of
lone pair regions.

00:40:51.833 --> 00:40:54.235
And we know about our rules,
and we know about bond order.

00:40:56.604 --> 00:41:00.575
So now if I look at those same--

00:41:00.575 --> 00:41:01.876
really?

00:41:01.876 --> 00:41:04.879
What happened to-- oh,
there they are up there.

00:41:04.879 --> 00:41:07.081
If I look at those
same ones, well, OK,

00:41:07.081 --> 00:41:09.951
look those are tetrahedral.

00:41:09.951 --> 00:41:13.087
OK, the electron
pair distribution

00:41:13.087 --> 00:41:17.192
is tetrahedral,
but-- gesundheit.

00:41:17.192 --> 00:41:19.561
But as you can see
in these pictures,

00:41:19.561 --> 00:41:22.897
you can get three
different molecular shapes.

00:41:22.897 --> 00:41:26.000
You can get tetrahedral,
same as the electron pair.

00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:29.137
You can get trigonal
pyramidal, or you can get bent.

00:41:29.137 --> 00:41:32.373
Those are the names
of the molecule.

00:41:32.373 --> 00:41:34.008
Notice that those
aren't the names that

00:41:34.008 --> 00:41:35.343
include the lone pairs.

00:41:35.343 --> 00:41:38.146
Lone pairs are how you find
which one of these categories

00:41:38.146 --> 00:41:40.215
you're in.

00:41:40.215 --> 00:41:41.916
But those are the
names of the molecules.

00:41:41.916 --> 00:41:43.284
Those are really
important, those

00:41:43.284 --> 00:41:45.687
names of the molecular shape.

00:41:45.687 --> 00:41:48.056
Now, if I move over one--

00:41:48.056 --> 00:41:50.892
OK, oh there they are,
names, tetrahedral, trigonal,

00:41:50.892 --> 00:41:52.026
pyramidal, bent.

00:41:52.026 --> 00:41:53.094
Now, let's move over one.

00:41:53.094 --> 00:41:54.162
So now I'm going to stop.

00:41:54.162 --> 00:41:57.832
I'm not going to keep on
writing everything down.

00:41:57.832 --> 00:41:58.967
Let's see one last example.

00:41:58.967 --> 00:42:01.102
So here's trigonal bipyramidal.

00:42:01.102 --> 00:42:04.472
This is what happens when
you have five electron pair

00:42:04.472 --> 00:42:06.474
domains.

00:42:06.474 --> 00:42:08.343
Again, and I keep
saying this, but I

00:42:08.343 --> 00:42:10.778
know that this can lead
to confusion later.

00:42:10.778 --> 00:42:15.850
So I keep on reiterating,
the electron payer domain

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:19.921
count, right, the five here,
the five here, which gives us

00:42:19.921 --> 00:42:23.691
this overall kind of electron
payer domain shape, that

00:42:23.691 --> 00:42:27.362
would be five there, right,
trigonal bipyramidal,

00:42:27.362 --> 00:42:33.268
that includes a single, double,
or triple bond is a domain.

00:42:33.268 --> 00:42:35.837
It's a BP.

00:42:35.837 --> 00:42:38.273
Right, it's a BP domain.

00:42:38.273 --> 00:42:40.408
A lone pair is a domain.

00:42:40.408 --> 00:42:43.645
How many of those do you have
floating around your molecule,

00:42:43.645 --> 00:42:44.746
around your atom?

00:42:44.746 --> 00:42:46.981
Sorry, your central atom.

00:42:46.981 --> 00:42:49.484
And then you go down and
you sort of decompose it

00:42:49.484 --> 00:42:54.289
in terms of how many of them are
bonding pairs, X's, versus how

00:42:54.289 --> 00:42:55.557
many of them are lone pairs.

00:42:55.557 --> 00:42:59.327
So let's do one last example.

