And this is, I think,
a nice example.

00:43:24.152 --> 00:43:33.895
If you had the molecule, if
you had the molecule SF4,

00:43:33.895 --> 00:43:35.630
I'll give you two
examples today that

00:43:35.630 --> 00:43:40.301
violate the octet rule,
right, because chemistry

00:43:40.301 --> 00:43:42.303
lives in the fast lane.

00:43:42.303 --> 00:43:49.344
If you have SF4, S is willing
to have an expanded octet,

00:43:49.344 --> 00:43:50.678
all right.

00:43:50.678 --> 00:43:52.714
As you go down in
the periodic table,

00:43:52.714 --> 00:43:56.751
atoms are more willing
to live in the fast lane

00:43:56.751 --> 00:43:58.353
and break the rules.

00:43:58.353 --> 00:44:05.827
S-- we've drawn it like
this, F, F, F, all right,

00:44:05.827 --> 00:44:08.029
we've drawn it like
this, violating

00:44:08.029 --> 00:44:09.664
the octet rule and
all that, but still

00:44:09.664 --> 00:44:12.266
being happy because
it's lowest in energy.

00:44:12.266 --> 00:44:17.305
And these are all my
electron non-binding domains.

00:44:22.510 --> 00:44:25.013
OK.

00:44:25.013 --> 00:44:27.849
If I count-- remember, those
aren't going to matter.

00:44:27.849 --> 00:44:28.783
They're on the outside.

00:44:28.783 --> 00:44:30.318
I'm picking one atom.

00:44:30.318 --> 00:44:35.490
A is S. Right, A
is S in this case.

00:44:35.490 --> 00:44:38.826
And in this case, I've got--

00:44:38.826 --> 00:44:41.095
you know, I've got, OK,
how many body pairs?

00:44:41.095 --> 00:44:47.669
I've got four bonding
pairs and one lone pair,

00:44:47.669 --> 00:44:49.504
and one lone pair.

00:44:49.504 --> 00:44:50.905
And so you can see--

00:44:50.905 --> 00:44:55.710
I can just look
this up here AX4E1.

00:44:55.710 --> 00:44:57.412
It must be a seesaw structure.

00:44:57.412 --> 00:44:59.547
But I want you to
see it, and this

00:44:59.547 --> 00:45:04.519
is where playing with these
models really helps you see it.

00:45:04.519 --> 00:45:11.459
Because if you think about
this, it seems, at first,

00:45:11.459 --> 00:45:15.496
that if I had four bonding
pairs and one lone pair,

00:45:15.496 --> 00:45:17.732
why can't I just
write it like this?

00:45:17.732 --> 00:45:21.402
This seems like a good
idea at first, right?

00:45:21.402 --> 00:45:25.707
S, and then you've got
a fluorine down here.

00:45:25.707 --> 00:45:28.176
And then you're going to go out.

00:45:28.176 --> 00:45:32.513
I'm using a kind of
cool new notation here.

00:45:32.513 --> 00:45:34.415
Right-- and then
you're going to go.

00:45:34.415 --> 00:45:36.017
Did somebody just say whoa?

00:45:36.017 --> 00:45:37.685
Or is that just in my mind?

00:45:37.685 --> 00:45:40.455
Like that, but I'm saying whoa.

00:45:40.455 --> 00:45:43.024
That's a terrible
rendition of this notation.

00:45:43.024 --> 00:45:49.197
But this means-- now I've
got to come on the board.

00:45:49.197 --> 00:45:51.099
And for me, that's
challenging because I'm not

00:45:51.099 --> 00:45:53.968
very good at drawing 3D things.

00:45:53.968 --> 00:45:58.873
And so we use these little
shaded in kind of sticks

00:45:58.873 --> 00:46:00.942
like that, and it
comes out of the board,

00:46:00.942 --> 00:46:02.443
and then that's
like the dashed one,

00:46:02.443 --> 00:46:03.544
and it goes into the board.

00:46:07.148 --> 00:46:09.050
So there's one--
that looks terrible.

00:46:12.220 --> 00:46:13.488
I'm going to try one more time.

00:46:13.488 --> 00:46:20.194
So maybe if I just do
this, F, F. And this

00:46:20.194 --> 00:46:22.163
is in the same plane here.

00:46:22.163 --> 00:46:24.599
These three are in a plane,
and that's going down.

00:46:24.599 --> 00:46:27.935
And you've got your
lone pair up here.

00:46:27.935 --> 00:46:30.304
That looks pretty good.

00:46:30.304 --> 00:46:36.844
Why is that better than
something like this?

00:46:36.844 --> 00:46:42.283
And now, one coming out of
the board and the other going

00:46:42.283 --> 00:46:46.954
into the board, all right, and
now this would be-- gesundheit.

00:46:46.954 --> 00:46:49.724
This would be my
other possibility.

00:46:49.724 --> 00:46:51.092
I mean, if I think
about, I think

00:46:51.092 --> 00:46:52.393
why can't it just be this?

00:46:52.393 --> 00:46:53.494
These look-- I don't know.

00:46:53.494 --> 00:46:55.096
These look nice and spread out.

00:46:55.096 --> 00:46:58.032
Why not?

00:46:58.032 --> 00:47:00.334
It's the lone pair again.

00:47:00.334 --> 00:47:02.336
It's the lone pair.

00:47:02.336 --> 00:47:03.771
It's got to be,
because, you know,

00:47:03.771 --> 00:47:09.944
I put up there, somewhere,
there's not there,

00:47:09.944 --> 00:47:13.781
that the lone pair
repels the bonds more,

00:47:13.781 --> 00:47:18.119
and I've got more than
say a bond and a bond.

00:47:18.119 --> 00:47:21.789
But the point is I've
got three of those here.

00:47:21.789 --> 00:47:27.895
This has three,
one, two, and three.

00:47:27.895 --> 00:47:29.697
This has two.

00:47:29.697 --> 00:47:32.400
This is a happier structure.

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:33.467
Right.

00:47:33.467 --> 00:47:36.470
So I wanted you to see-- so this
is the way to think about it.

00:47:36.470 --> 00:47:38.072
You're using VSEPR.

00:47:38.072 --> 00:47:41.876
You're applying
these principles.

00:47:41.876 --> 00:47:44.712
I mean, it's just a
four-point recipe,

00:47:44.712 --> 00:47:46.914
but I'm hoping that in
thinking about it this way

00:47:46.914 --> 00:47:48.916
and having the little
models in your hand,

00:47:48.916 --> 00:47:51.953
which you have in
this goody bag,

00:47:51.953 --> 00:47:56.557
that you guys will also kind
of have a feeling for it,

00:47:56.557 --> 00:47:58.559
so that yes, you
have the tables that

00:47:58.559 --> 00:48:02.363
are written out much better
than how I wrote them out.

00:48:02.363 --> 00:48:04.966
And here's one in the lecture
notes that I'll leave you,

00:48:04.966 --> 00:48:06.801
right, total domains.

00:48:06.801 --> 00:48:12.540
It's very similar, notation,
structures, OK, shapes.

00:48:12.540 --> 00:48:17.211
But also that you have a feeling
for why this is the case.

00:48:17.211 --> 00:48:19.680
All right, shapes of molecules.

00:48:19.680 --> 00:48:25.620
See you guys on
Wednesday-- no, Friday.

